  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 PM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nHumid but otherwise pleasant weather this afternoon. Humidity will increase slightly tomorrow, \nwhile we wait for an incoming cold front to provide some marginal\nrelief and knock us back down to regular summer conditions. Heat \nindices in the southern valley tomorrow afternoon should once \nagain climb into the low 100sF. We will likely be near heat \nadvisory criteria for portions of the valley south of Interstate \n40, at or around 105F feels like. \n\nNot feeling very confident about the degree to which we see \nsevere thunderstorms tomorrow. Certainly from a parameter space, \nthe overall numbers are favorable between supportive overall \nshear, a couple thousand MLCAPE, and enough time between morning \ncloud cover and destabilization in the afternoon hours. On the \nother hand, overall upper dynamics aren\u2019t too favorable for severe\nweather, the morning remnants of the storms upstream of us may \ninduce a backside sinking motion in the atmosphere, which could \nthen inhibit updraft strength later in the day. The HREF only has \nvery low probabilities for strong updraft speeds and experimental \nAI guidance along with traditional CAMs are also struggling to \ndepict widespread convective coverage. There\u2019s a few model s that \nare active, namely the Euro, RRFS, MPAS. The main threat with this\nwill be damaging wind gusts with discrete thunderstorms. I think \na tornado threat is very low, between probably too high LCLs and \ntrending weaker low level helicity, that\u2019s not as big on my radar\nas yesterday. More objective HREF and experimental guidance also \nkeep probabilities really low but the ML nadocast is still \ncarrying a 2% tornado risk, so again wouldn\u2019t rule it out \noutright. Otherwise, if storms are able to fire and reach their \nfull potential, the environment is prone for damaging winds 60 to \n70 mph, fueled by midlevel dry air and plenty of CAPE to power \nstrong downdrafts. Small to maybe 1\u201d hail as well, though the high\nfreezing heights of July will be melting any hail falling. \n\nThere\u2019s potential for morning showers and maybe a thunderstorm early \ntomorrow morning, but guidance has been consistent in weakening \nthese storms or outright dissipation. Then there\u2019s a nonzero risk \nwe see scattered convection in a northwest to southeast direction \nthrough the day over southwest Virginia, but again, low \nconfidence. The upper jet looking its best at 06z in the morning \nis not the greatest sign for significant severe. Overall the risk \nfor severe weather is low, and in the narrow timeframe of say 2 pm\nto 6 pm EDT, mainly focused in northeastern Tennessee and \nsouthwestern Virginia. \n\nBeyond Tuesday we\u2019ll have rain chances diminishing in the mountains \novernight with the passage of the cold front, and then a drier \nair mass leads to regular summer vibes through the back half of \nthe week with likely dry weather. A new upper trough arrives in \nthe Eastern US by this Saturday, and it\u2019s this trough that will \nalso bring about the next bout of rain chances in time for the \nweekend.",
